The interface files in this directory illustrate how the XRT/graph and
XRT/3d widgets can be used with XFaceMaker.

To use these demos, you must have the XRT widgets installed and a valid
XRT license file. Your XRTHOME environment variable must be properly set.
If you do not already have an extended XFaceMaker executable that includes
the XRT/Graph and XRT/3d extensions, you will have to build one, in order
to be able to load the interface files.

Do not forget to set your XRTHOME environment variable before you build 
your extended XFaceMaker. If you do not have access to both the graph
and 3d widgets, build your XFaceMaker with the extension corresponding
to the widget you have access to. 

Once you have built your extended XFaceMaker, add the path to reach it
to your PATH environment variable, and cd to this directory.

These demos will work correctly only if the current directory for XFaceMaker
is the directory where the files reside, because the data files are
assumed to be local.

XRT/3d demo

Load the xrt3d.fm file into XFaceMaker. To interact with the display,
switch to TRY mode, and use mouse button 2 to rotate the viewing cube.
You can have a look at the resource settings, you can generate the
application files and build the application, if you want to verify that
the Makefile generated is correct and that all the libraries are
accessible.

XRT/graph demo

The interface lets the user change the value of a point on the graph by
dragging it, attach a text message to a point, add points to the graph,
and output the graph as either a data file or a PostScript file.

Load the xrtgraph.fm file into XFaceMaker.


--> To drag points on the graph, use mouse button 1 (the Select button).
The interface sets the XmNtranslations resource of the XRT/graph widget
to process events on this button. Note that if the type of data in the 
graph is XRT_DATA_ARRAY, the x-value of a point cannot change independently 
for a  given set of data (a line on the graph), but changes for all sets 
of data at the same time.
You might want to look at the translation scripts for <Btn1Down>, 
<Btn1Motion> and <Btn1Up> to see how point motion is implemented.

--> To attach text to a point, use mouse button 3 (the Custom button).
If you press the Custom mouse button over a point in the graph, the 
interface attaches a small text item to the point under the cursor. 
If you press the Custom mouse button anywhere else in the graph, the 
widget removes any previously attached text.
You might want to look at the translation script for <Btn3Down> text
attachment is implemented.

--> To add points to the graph, you will use the Add button.
The procedure depends on the type of data being displayed.
If the type returned by the function XrtGetDataType is 
XRT_DATA_ARRAY (the type initially loaded), here is how to enter a 
data point:
	Specify, in the 'x' input field, the x value of the points 
	you want to add. In the 'y' input field, specify the new y value.
	All the curves will have the same value for the point added, since 
	this simple interface has only one entry field for all the y
	values (it was developed with data type XRT_DATA_GENERAL in mind).
	Click Add.

If the data type is XRT_DATA_GENERAL here is how to specify a data
point (to have access to this type of data, you have to create a
new empty array with the New General button - see below):
	Enter the set number in the 'set' input field (set number values are
vary from 0 to nset-1).
	Enter the x value in the 'x' input field.
	Enter the y value in the 'y' input field.
	Click the Add button.

--> To clear the graph, create a new set of data with no points, by 
clicking the New Array or New General button. 
The New Array button creates empty data of type XRT_DATA_ARRAY.
Specify the number of sets in the data in the 'set' input field before
you click on the New Array button, or the number of sets will be 1.

The New General button creates empty data of type XRT_DATA_GENERAL.
Specify the number of setts in the data in the 'set' input field before
you click on the New General button, or the number of sets will be 1.

--> To save the graph, click on the 'Save' button. A data file called
'outputdata' is saved in the current directory. This uses the function
XrtDataHandleOutputData.

--> To create a PostScript file of the data, click on the 'Draw' button.
A postscript file called 'xrtoutput.ps' is created in the current 
directory. You can print the file on a postscript printer, or use a
postscript viewer such as 'gs' to view the  file.
